\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The law on a selected subspace}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For a \vocabk{selected background}{fdip-005A}, view #{P} as a map
#{H\to X=\operatorname{im}P} and let #{r=\dim X}. Write
#{\iota_X:X\to H} for the inclusion of the image. The counting inner
product identifies #{X} with its dual, so the compressed operators
below are endomorphisms of #{X}. The law #{\nu_b=P_*\mu_Q} is the
centered Gaussian on #{X} with covariance and precision
##{C_P=\iota_X^{\dagger}\,G\,\iota_X:X\to X,\qquad S_P=C_P^{-1}.}
The covariance #{C_P} is positive definite. Relative to the induced Euclidean volume #{\lambda_X}, its density is
##{\frac{d\nu_b}{d\lambda_X}(x)
 =\frac{\sqrt{\det_X S_P}}{(2\pi)^{r/2}}
   \ue^{-\langle x,S_Px\rangle /2}.}
An isometric coordinate basis and the change among such bases are
recorded in \ref{fdip-00LL}. The operator #{S_P} does not depend
on that basis.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{B:\mathbb R^r\to H} be an isometric inclusion with image
#{X}, so #{BB^{\dagger}=P}. Transporting the domain of #{B} along this
isometry identifies #{B} with #{\iota_X}. For nonzero #{x\in X},
#{\langle x,C_Px\rangle=\langle x,Gx\rangle>0}.
Set #{C=B^{\dagger}GB}, so #{C>0}. The positive square roots of #{Q}
and #{C} exist by the real spectral theorem. The density
change of variables #{z=Q^{1/2}\phi} gives the standard Gaussian
density #{(2\pi)^{-|V|/2}\ue^{-|z|^2/2}}: its Jacobian and the
[normalizer](fdip-002T) cancel. Put
##{T=C^{-1/2}B^{\dagger}Q^{-1/2}.}
Then #{TT^{\dagger}=C^{-1/2}(B^{\dagger}GB)C^{-1/2}=\bbI_r}, so its rows extend
to an orthonormal basis of #{H}. The resulting square orthogonal
matrix carries #{z} to a standard Gaussian, since its Jacobian
has absolute value one and it preserves the squared norm.
Its first #{r} coordinates are #{Tz}; integration over the other
coordinates gives the standard #{r}-dimensional density. Hence
##{B^{\dagger}\phi=C^{1/2}Tz}
has density #{(2\pi)^{-r/2}(\det C)^{-1/2}
\ue^{-y^TC^{-1}y/2}} by an invertible change of variables.
The isometry #{B} carries Euclidean volume to #{\lambda_X} and
#{B(B^{\dagger}\phi)=P\phi}. This proves the law and its density, rather
than inferring a law from its covariance alone.}

\p{For #{x,x'\in X}, the covariance of the corresponding linear
observations is #{\langle x,Gx'\rangle=\langle x,C_Px'\rangle}
by the [inverse-precision formula](fdip-000B). The matrices of
#{C_P} and #{S_P} in the basis #{B}, and their change under
#{B'=BU}, are the identities in \ref{fdip-00LL}.}
}

\p{In the orthogonal split #{H=X\oplus Y}, write #{\iota_Y:Y\to H}
for the inclusion of the complement. The blocks of #{Q} are the
compositions
##{A=\iota_X^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_X,\qquad K=\iota_X^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_Y,\qquad D=\iota_Y^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_Y.}
Since #{Q>0}, the operator #{D} is positive definite. Then
##{S_P=A-KD^{-1}K^{\dagger}.}
The #{Y}-component of #{Q(\iota_Xx+\iota_Yy)=\iota_Xu} is #{K^{\dagger}x+Dy=0},
so #{y=-D^{-1}K^{\dagger}x}, and the #{X}-component is
#{(A-KD^{-1}K^{\dagger})x=u}. Positivity follows by evaluating the
quadratic form on #{\iota_Xx-\iota_YD^{-1}K^{\dagger}x}. Thus #{C_P} is the
inverse of this Schur complement. For #{P=P_R}, this recovers
the [coordinate marginal](fdip-000E). If #{PQ=QP}, then #{K=0}
and #{S_P} is the compression of #{Q} to #{X}; symmetry of a
subspace alone does not justify that equality. The matrices of
#{A}, #{K} and #{D} in a pair of orthonormal bases are in
\ref{fdip-00LL}.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.2, p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}
gives formulas (A.4)-(A.6) for Gaussian densities and marginals.}

\p{\citet{Definition 8.3, p. 384}{friedli2017statistical}
characterizes Gaussian vectors by linear observations.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 8.4, p. 385}{friedli2017statistical}
gives their nonsingular density. The row-isometry argument above supplies the
linear-image step, including its Euclidean-volume identification.}


